{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass, as he finished.</b> The reason for his saying <b>וַיְהִי</b> is in the language of distress, for the Torah grieves over the fact that he finished without blessing with all the blessings — for we find that he blessed Esau afterward, and if he had blessed him with the remaining blessings, the hope of Edom would already have been lost." "<b>And it came to pass, as soon as he had gone out, etc.</b> The reason for his saying <b>וַיְהִי</b> is in the language of distress; also he said אַךְ, and he also doubled the expression, saying יָצֹא יָצָא. It intends to say that Jacob's departure was still being delayed, and this is the meaning of <b>אַךְ יָצֹא</b> — he qualified the departure, [indicating] it was only for a reason that occurred: 'gone out.' And the reason is what Scripture informs at the end, in its saying ״וְעֵשָׂו אָחִיו בָּא מִצֵּידוֹ״, and for this reason he hurried to complete the departure, which is his saying <b>יָצָא</b>. And he alluded to the distress that Jacob felt — that he might be found as a thief in the tunnel before his father when he sensed Esau's arrival. And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah, chapter 66) interpreted it in another manner."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:43:56.857097174Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}